                <pb facs="0366" n="276" sameAs="frus:frus1932v02/pg276"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.00/2645a</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Division of Western European Affairs
                            (Boal) to the
                        Ambassador in Germany (Sackett)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-10">December 10, 1931</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: I am enclosing for your
                    information a copy of a memorandum which sets up a few observations with regard
                    to the present course of events in Germany. These observations while based for
                    the most part on newspaper reports seem to us in the Department to be fairly
                    accurate. We have not had any reports from the Embassy which have covered this
                    question in just the same way as this memorandum approaches it. For our own
                    information here in the Department, I would appreciate it if you could give me
                    your views with regard to this memorandum with any amplification or added
                    information which you might deem pertinent.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Pierre de L.
                                Boal</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d191-a1.xml" xpointer="d191-a1"/>
